WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:09.433 line:88% align:center
Checkpoint drugs work by releasing the brakes on a T cell, but they help only a minority of patients, because only a handful of those brakes are drugged.

2
00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:13.271 line:88% align:center
So we went looking for the rest of them, right across the genome.

3
00:00:13.271 --> 00:00:15.987 line:88% align:center
This is Brakepoint, built with Claude Science.

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.171 line:88% align:center
Those brakes matter beyond checkpoint therapy.

5
00:00:19.171 --> 00:00:21.339 line:88% align:center
They also throttle engineered CAR-T.

6
00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:24.291 line:88% align:center
So across the genome, which genes are the brakes?

7
00:00:24.291 --> 00:00:28.748 line:88% align:center
And which knockdowns push a human T cell toward a stronger effector state?

8
00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:37.856 line:88% align:center
6 million primary human CD4 T cells, from the Gladstone Institutes.

9
00:00:37.856 --> 00:00:42.525 line:88% align:center
Two donors, out of an intended four.

10
00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:46.617 line:88% align:center
How do you find the brakes in twelve thousand knockdowns?

11
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:51.917 line:88% align:center
The reflex is to rank by significance, but at two million cells, that breaks down.

12
00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:55.019 line:88% align:center
Over 97% of the tested knockdowns clear the bar.

13
00:00:55.019 --> 00:00:57.669 line:88% align:center
So we rank by causal effect size instead.

14
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.324 line:88% align:center
But effect size alone still points at the wrong genes.

15
00:01:01.324 --> 00:01:04.667 line:88% align:center
The biggest hits are the cell's own signaling machinery.

16
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:07.413 line:88% align:center
So we add what a magnitude ranking leaves out.

17
00:01:07.413 --> 00:01:08.010 line:88% align:center
Direction.

18
00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.652 line:88% align:center
Does the knockdown push toward the effector program, or away?

19
00:01:11.652 --> 00:01:15.830 line:88% align:center
Now the machinery falls away, and the candidate brakes rise into view.

20
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:18.161 line:88% align:center
And this isn't a static picture.

21
00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:23.342 line:88% align:center
Every one of the eleven thousand that passed our testing threshold is here to explore.

22
00:01:23.342 --> 00:01:28.101 line:88% align:center
Search any gene, hover any point, and read its causal effect and its direction.

23
00:01:28.101 --> 00:01:30.028 line:88% align:center
It's the real leaderboard, live.

24
00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:31.636 line:88% align:center
And that's the edge.

25
00:01:31.636 --> 00:01:35.306 line:88% align:center
Differential expression finds correlations, not what to drug.

26
00:01:35.306 --> 00:01:38.133 line:88% align:center
Genetics points to a locus, rarely a direction.

27
00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:44.991 line:88% align:center
We measure what a knockdown actually does, and which way it pushes, then weigh it against genetics and the clinic.

28
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:52.114 line:88% align:center
From that map, five candidates chosen using prior evidence: CBLB, CD5, DGKA, SMAD3, and UBASH3A.

29
00:01:52.114 --> 00:02:00.367 line:88% align:center
Each scored across seven lines of evidence, from causal effect and direction to human genetics and clinical precedent.

30
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:01.938 line:88% align:center
Our lead is CBLB.

31
00:02:01.938 --> 00:02:08.500 line:88% align:center
It's a natural off-switch for T-cell activation, and inhibitors are already in early-phase trials.

32
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:13.053 line:88% align:center
Its genetics point the same way, and it lands in our brake quadrant.

33
00:02:13.053 --> 00:02:14.593 line:88% align:center
CD5 and DGKA come next.

34
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.906 line:88% align:center
We're honest about what two donors can support.

35
00:02:17.906 --> 00:02:19.699 line:88% align:center
CD5 and DGKA hold up in both.

36
00:02:19.699 --> 00:02:21.802 line:88% align:center
The other three ride on one donor.

37
00:02:21.802 --> 00:02:27.862 line:88% align:center
Known brakes aren't significantly enriched, but that's a two-donor power limit, not a null result.

38
00:02:27.862 --> 00:02:33.922 line:88% align:center
So this stays a ranked shortlist for the full cohort, a hypothesis to test, not a finished answer.

39
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:36.484 line:88% align:center
Five candidate brakes, led by CBLB.

40
00:02:36.484 --> 00:02:41.400 line:88% align:center
It's a target the industry already drugs, so recovering it validates our method.

41
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:48.159 line:88% align:center
Every figure regenerates from cached outputs with one command, checked against a real bug we caught ourselves.

42
00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:53.567 line:88% align:center
Explore the live map, clone the code, and help find the brakes today's drugs still miss.

43
